export const meta = {
  name: 'sandbox-exec-gap-audit',
  description: 'Root-cause the Vercel sandbox exec 429 and enumerate agent-tooling gaps for consolidation',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Investigate', detail: 'parallel readers over exec path, rate limits, tooling contract' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'adversarially check each finding against the code' },
    { title: 'Synthesize', detail: 'consolidated gap list + simplification proposal' },
  ],
}

const ROOT = '/Users/burakemre/Code/lobu/.claude/worktrees/nix-packages-allowlist'

const FINDING_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string' },
          file: { type: 'string' },
          line: { type: 'number' },
          evidence: { type: 'string', description: 'exact code or comment quoted from the file' },
          why_it_breaks: { type: 'string' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocker', 'major', 'minor'] },
        },
        required: ['title', 'file', 'evidence', 'why_it_breaks', 'severity'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['findings'],
}

const LENSES = [
  {
    key: 'exec-429',
    prompt: `In ${ROOT}, read packages/server/src/gateway/runtime/providers/vercel.ts, focusing on the exec() method around line 286-325.

HYPOTHESIS TO VERIFY OR REFUTE: every exec() call issues "await sandbox.fs.mkdir(REMOTE_WORKSPACE_DIR, {recursive:true})" BEFORE runCommand. Under load this extra API call gets HTTP 429 rate-limited from Vercel, throws, and surfaces to the agent as a shell error — so even a trivial "echo hello > /tmp/x.txt" reports "Status code 429". OBSERVED IN PRODUCTION: a plain echo returned 429.

Also check: getSandbox() is called per-exec too — does it create or re-fetch on every command? Is there any caching, retry, or backoff? Does any error from mkdir/getSandbox get converted into a user-facing shell error rather than a distinguishable infrastructure error?

Report concrete findings with exact quoted code and line numbers.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'error-surfacing',
    prompt: `In ${ROOT}, trace how an infrastructure error from the remote runtime provider reaches the agent's bash tool output.

Start at packages/server/src/gateway/runtime/providers/vercel.ts exec(), then find the caller (grep for RuntimeExecContext, runtime-route, generic-runtime-bash) and follow to where the agent sees a result.

KEY QUESTION: when the provider throws (network, 429, auth), does the agent see a distinguishable "infrastructure failure" or does it look like the COMMAND failed? This matters enormously: an agent told a command failed will retry/improvise, while one told the infrastructure failed can report it. Production evidence: an agent concluded "printf failed with Status code 429... it's possible the shell misinterpreted it" and then invented workarounds.

Report exact code paths and line numbers.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'tooling-contract',
    prompt: `In ${ROOT}, audit the connector agentTooling contract for consolidation opportunities.

Read: packages/connector-sdk/src/connector-types.ts (ConnectorAgentTooling), packages/server/src/gateway/agent-tooling/resolver.ts, packages/server/src/gateway/agent-tooling/credential-lease.ts, packages/connectors/src/github.ts (around line 344).

Questions: (1) The contract declares nix.packages + env + domains. env and domains are self-delivering, but nix.packages is only a REQUEST that something must act on — where is that asymmetry documented and is it handled? (2) credential-lease.ts mintFor returns null on failure and resolver.ts does "if (!lease) continue" — the failure is logged and dropped. What does the agent see? (3) Is there ANY mechanism that tells the agent what tooling/credentials it has? Grep for a capability manifest, system-prompt injection of tooling, or similar.

Report what exists, what is missing, and what could be consolidated.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'sandbox-lifecycle',
    prompt: `In ${ROOT}, determine the Vercel sandbox lifecycle and whether filesystem state persists.

Read packages/server/src/gateway/runtime/providers/vercel.ts fully, especially stableSandboxName() (~line 96-122) and getSandbox().

OBSERVED IN PRODUCTION: a file written to /tmp in one conversation turn was GONE in the next turn ("I need the content of /tmp/body.json to run the curl command"). Also: sandbox identity hashes organizationId:agentId:conversationId.

Questions: is the sandbox per-conversation persistent or recreated per exec? What does sandbox.persistent mean here? Is there a snapshot/retention mechanism (grep sameSnapshotRetention, keepLastSnapshots)? If sandboxes ARE persistent, why did /tmp not survive — different sandbox, or a reset?

This determines whether multi-step agent work (write file, then use it) is possible at all. Report with exact code.`,
  },
]

phase('Investigate')

const results = await pipeline(
  LENSES,
  (lens) => agent(lens.prompt, { label: `probe:${lens.key}`, phase: 'Investigate', schema: FINDING_SCHEMA }),
  (res, lens) => {
    if (!res || !res.findings || res.findings.length === 0) return []
    return parallel(
      res.findings.map((f) => () =>
        agent(
          `Adversarially verify this claim about the codebase at ${ROOT}. Your DEFAULT is refuted=true; only accept it if you can quote the code yourself.

CLAIM: ${f.title}
FILE: ${f.file}${f.line ? ':' + f.line : ''}
EVIDENCE OFFERED: ${f.evidence}
WHY IT SUPPOSEDLY BREAKS: ${f.why_it_breaks}

Open the file. Verify the quoted code actually exists and actually means what is claimed. A claim that is merely plausible, or that describes intended behaviour rather than a real defect, must be refuted. Report refuted=true/false with your own quoted evidence.`,
          {
            label: `verify:${lens.key}`,
            phase: 'Verify',
            schema: {
              type: 'object',
              properties: {
                refuted: { type: 'boolean' },
                own_evidence: { type: 'string' },
                note: { type: 'string' },
              },
              required: ['refuted', 'own_evidence'],
            },
          }
        ).then((v) => ({ ...f, lens: lens.key, verdict: v }))
      )
    )
  }
)

const all = results.flat().filter(Boolean)
const confirmed = all.filter((f) => f.verdict && f.verdict.refuted === false)

log(`${all.length} findings raised, ${confirmed.length} survived adversarial verification`)

phase('Synthesize')

const synthesis = await agent(
  `You are consolidating a gap audit of the Lobu agent-tooling and remote-sandbox stack at ${ROOT}.

CONFIRMED FINDINGS (each independently verified against the code):
${JSON.stringify(confirmed.map((f) => ({ title: f.title, file: f.file, line: f.line, evidence: f.evidence, why: f.why_it_breaks, severity: f.severity })), null, 2)}

PRODUCTION CONTEXT, all observed live today:
- The GitHub App lease WORKS: agent bash in a Vercel sandbox holds a real ghs_ token; GET /repos/lobu-ai/landing (private) returned 200; POST /git/refs CREATED a branch. Credential + egress + authenticated write are proven.
- BLOCKED: any command writing a file fails. "echo hello > /tmp/x.txt && cat /tmp/x.txt" returns isError with "Status code 429". A shell redirect cannot produce an HTTP status, so this is infrastructure leaking through.
- /tmp does NOT persist between conversation turns.
- The agent, when tooling was missing, FABRICATED a "simulated pull request" rather than reporting failure.
- gh is absent from the sandbox image; /nix does not exist; cache.nixos.org is DNS-blocked. So agents CANNOT self-provision nix packages today.
- Egress filtering is correct: api.github.com 200, example.com blocked.
- A merged fix (#2274) added NIX_PACKAGES to the worker env allowlist; it now reaches the sandbox but nothing installs the binary.

The user's stated direction: connectors should expose env + nix packages so agents can be TOLD what they can use; they want to remove tech debt, simplify and consolidate — not add workarounds. They rejected a "degraded[] side-channel" as a bolt-on.

Produce:
1. ROOT CAUSE of the 429-on-echo, stated in one paragraph with the exact code responsible.
2. A ranked gap list (blocker first). For each: the one-line fix direction. Merge duplicates ruthlessly.
3. A CONSOLIDATION proposal: what single coherent change would subsume several of these gaps at once? Be concrete about files and shape. Explicitly say what gets DELETED, not just added.
4. What should NOT be built, and why.

Be concrete and short. No filler.`,
  { label: 'synthesize', phase: 'Synthesize' }
)

return { confirmedCount: confirmed.length, totalRaised: all.length, synthesis }

